WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
In 1847, a Vienna hospital had two maternity

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.600
clinics.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.280
In the doctors' clinic, about one mother in

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.080
ten died of a fever after giving birth.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.200
In the midwives' clinic, far fewer died.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.400
Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that the doctors came straight

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.840
from autopsies.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.200
He made them wash their hands in a

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.000
chlorine solution, and deaths fell to about the

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.680
midwives' level.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.880
So how can washing, or keeping people apart,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.120
stop a disease nobody could see?

00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:34.093
A pathogen has to leave one host, travel,

00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:36.573
and enter a next host it can infect.

00:00:36.894 --> 00:00:39.173
Every control measure breaks a link.

00:00:39.454 --> 00:00:41.453
Here's the link each one breaks.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:43.933
Hygiene cuts the travel link.

00:00:44.254 --> 00:00:48.093
Isolation and quarantine stop pathogens leaving infected people.

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:52.093
And vaccination removes hosts the pathogen can infect.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.540
In Vienna, the travel link was the doctors'

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:57.380
hands.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.300
Material from the autopsies carried bacteria, and hands

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.660
carried them to the mothers.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.820
The chlorine solution killed them.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.620
Today, soap and water do the job.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.500
Soap lifts oil, and the microbes stuck in

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.980
it, off the skin, and the water rinses

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:13.940
them away.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.300
Alcohol hand rub kills many microbes, but it

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.460
doesn't reliably kill norovirus or bacterial spores, so

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.020
soap and water work better against those.

00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:29.473
Hygiene also works on places, not just hands.

00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:33.953
Cleaning surfaces, cooking food properly, treating drinking water

00:01:34.034 --> 00:01:36.753
and carrying sewage away all cut the travel

00:01:36.874 --> 00:01:38.993
link for touch, food and water.

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:43.153
Changing places like this is called environmental management.

00:01:43.714 --> 00:01:46.673
Treated water and sewers keep working without anyone

00:01:46.794 --> 00:01:47.793
having to remember.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.040
But hygiene can't break every link.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.360
Measles spreads through the air.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.880
An infected person breathes out tiny droplets carrying

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.040
the virus, and the virus can stay in

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.320
the air of a room for up to

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.840
two hours.

00:02:02.947 --> 00:02:05.560
Washing your hands does little against that.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.800
To stop measles, you have to keep people

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.200
apart, or make them immune.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.080
Keeping infected people away from others is called

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.120
isolation.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.560
A person with measles is isolated until four

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.800
days after the rash appears.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.480
But there's a catch.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.040
They're infectious from about four days before the

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.920
rash, so by the time anyone knows, they

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.440
may already have spread it.

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:35.186
So the case's contacts, the people who shared

00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:37.026
the air, are traced.

00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:39.346
This is contact tracing.

00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:42.946
Some contacts who aren't immune need quarantine, depending

00:02:42.987 --> 00:02:45.106
on their exposure and setting.

00:02:45.147 --> 00:02:47.186
They're kept away from others, even though they

00:02:47.227 --> 00:02:50.226
aren't sick, because they might be infected.

00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:52.866
Quarantine lasts about as long as the incubation

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:55.986
period, the time from infection to symptoms.

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:59.266
For measles, that's up to about eighteen days.

00:02:59.347 --> 00:03:01.986
If they get sick, they move into isolation.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Quarantine works at borders too.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.800
Australia's biosecurity system checks travellers, cargo and imported

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.000
animals, to keep out pests and diseases.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.000
And in 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, Australia

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.600
made returning travellers quarantine for fourteen days.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.960
A vaccinated person usually has memory cells, so

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.560
the pathogen can't use them.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.600
And when enough people are immune, an infected

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.000
person meets too few people who can catch

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:34.320
it.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.520
Each case infects fewer than one new person

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.320
on average, so the outbreak fizzles out.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.560
This is herd immunity.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.000
It also protects people who can't be vaccinated,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.640
like babies too young for the measles vaccine.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.226
The more contagious a disease is, the more

00:03:53.307 --> 00:03:54.586
people need to be immune.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.786
Where nobody is immune, one measles case can

00:03:57.867 --> 00:03:59.906
infect about fifteen people.

00:04:00.187 --> 00:04:02.746
To get that below one, more than fourteen

00:04:02.827 --> 00:04:05.306
in every fifteen people need to be immune.

00:04:05.707 --> 00:04:07.546
That's about ninety-three per cent.

00:04:08.107 --> 00:04:11.466
Health services aim for ninety-five, because no vaccine

00:04:11.547 --> 00:04:13.466
protects every person who gets it.

00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:15.546
And local coverage matters.

00:04:16.027 --> 00:04:18.986
If vaccination drops in one school or town,

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:21.226
measles brought in by a traveller can spread

00:04:21.347 --> 00:04:21.706
there.

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:25.493
So how do you judge a measure?

00:04:26.134 --> 00:04:28.773
Ask which link it breaks, how fast it

00:04:28.854 --> 00:04:31.173
works, how long it lasts, and what it

00:04:31.254 --> 00:04:32.613
costs people.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.733
Hygiene is cheap and lasting, but it mostly

00:04:35.814 --> 00:04:39.893
stops routes through hands, surfaces, food and water.

00:04:40.014 --> 00:04:43.093
Isolation and quarantine work fast for diseases spread

00:04:43.174 --> 00:04:45.893
between people, but they're hard to keep up,

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:48.053
and they only work if cases and contacts

00:04:48.134 --> 00:04:49.253
are found.

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:52.453
Vaccination lasts, but it needs a vaccine and

00:04:52.614 --> 00:04:53.773
high coverage.

00:04:53.814 --> 00:04:56.133
So outbreaks are controlled with several measures at

00:04:56.214 --> 00:04:56.773
once.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.080
Two ideas to keep.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.920
Isolate the infected, quarantine the exposed.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.280
And for herd immunity, break enough links and

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.720
the chain dies out.

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:12.173
Two exam questions.

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:13.213
First.

00:05:13.774 --> 00:05:16.333
A new virus has an incubation period of

00:05:16.414 --> 00:05:19.213
five to fourteen days, and people can spread

00:05:19.294 --> 00:05:21.693
it for two days before their symptoms start.

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.893
A town's plan says contacts will be quarantined

00:05:25.014 --> 00:05:27.693
for seven days, and anyone with symptoms will

00:05:27.734 --> 00:05:28.653
be isolated.

00:05:29.134 --> 00:05:30.333
Evaluate the plan.

00:05:30.974 --> 00:05:32.253
Pause here to answer.

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.786
The plan is only partly effective.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:48.986
Isolating people with symptoms is right, because it

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:51.066
removes known sources.

00:05:51.147 --> 00:05:53.386
But seven days is too short.

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:55.306
A contact could fall sick as late as

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.866
day fourteen, and spread the virus from day

00:05:58.027 --> 00:06:01.546
twelve, long after being released on day seven.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:04.746
Quarantine should last fourteen days.

00:06:04.827 --> 00:06:07.466
Saying quarantine is for people with symptoms is

00:06:07.587 --> 00:06:08.906
the classic mistake.

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:11.493
Second.

00:06:11.894 --> 00:06:15.133
For disease Y, herd immunity needs about eighty

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:16.853
per cent of people to be immune.

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:20.453
In town A, ninety-two per cent are immune.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:23.253
In town B, seventy per cent are.

00:06:23.974 --> 00:06:26.373
A visitor with disease Y spends a day

00:06:26.454 --> 00:06:27.493
in each town.

00:06:28.134 --> 00:06:30.533
Explain why an outbreak is more likely in

00:06:30.614 --> 00:06:33.093
town B, and why a baby too young

00:06:33.134 --> 00:06:36.133
to be vaccinated, with no antibodies against disease

00:06:36.294 --> 00:06:38.853
Y, is protected in town A.

00:06:39.494 --> 00:06:40.933
Pause here to answer.

00:06:52.134 --> 00:06:55.053
In town B, immunity is below the eighty

00:06:55.094 --> 00:06:57.653
per cent needed, so each case meets enough

00:06:57.734 --> 00:06:59.813
people who can catch it to infect more

00:06:59.934 --> 00:07:02.893
than one on average, and the outbreak grows.

00:07:03.174 --> 00:07:06.213
In town A, it's above eighty, so chains

00:07:06.294 --> 00:07:08.613
usually die out before they reach the baby.

00:07:09.334 --> 00:07:11.733
Saying the baby is immune is the classic

00:07:11.814 --> 00:07:12.693
mistake.

00:07:12.774 --> 00:07:15.413
It has no memory cells or antibodies against

00:07:15.494 --> 00:07:16.293
Y.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.373
The people around it are immune.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.600
So for any control question, name the route,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.840
find the link, and judge the measure on

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.160
speed, how long it lasts, and cost.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.440
Next, why antibiotics and antivirals work differently.

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:36.906
Here are the key notes.

00:07:37.147 --> 00:07:39.066
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:39.147 --> 00:07:39.946
to copy them.
